15:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1d",null,{"guide":{"date":"May 30–June 1, 1864","place":"Old Cold Harbor, Hanover County, Virginia","result":"Sheridan’s cavalry seized and retained the Cold Harbor crossroads; a poorly coordinated Confederate infantry attack failed before Wright’s VI Corps arrived","facts":[{"label":"Roads meeting at Old Cold Harbor","value":"5"},{"label":"Distance to New Cold Harbor","value":"About 1 mile"},{"label":"Smith’s XVIII Corps present","value":"About 10,000 men"},{"label":"Torbert’s cavalry division","value":"About 3,000 troopers"},{"label":"Sheridan’s total cavalry","value":"No more than 5,000"},{"label":"Kershaw and Hoke combined","value":"About 10,000 men"},{"label":"20th South Carolina strength","value":"About 1,100 men"}],"maps":[{"title":"Cold Harbor and the Chickahominy crossings","caption":"The historical battle map shows the five-road crossroads east of Richmond. Holding Cold Harbor protected the direct U.S. route toward the Chickahominy River and the James River beyond it.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/gvhs01.vhs00233/","image":"/images/cold-harbor-june-1-3-map.jpg","alt":"Historical map showing Cold Harbor, roads, rivers, and later battle lines"},{"title":"Cold Harbor, White House Landing, and Richmond","caption":"Smith’s XVIII Corps landed on the Pamunkey River at White House Landing and marched toward Cold Harbor. The crossroads stood between the U.S. supply base and the southeastern approaches to Richmond.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-77.72%2C37.48%2C-76.95%2C37.83&layer=mapnik&marker=37.5892%2C-77.2886"},{"title":"The southeastern turn of the Overland campaign","caption":"After the North Anna and Totopotomoy operations, Grant continued around Lee’s right. Cold Harbor lay on the route toward the lower Chickahominy crossings.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448882/","image":"/images/overland-campaign-1864-map.jpg","alt":"Historical map of Grant’s Overland campaign route toward Richmond and Petersburg"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:19","audioTime":79,"title":"Grant continued southeast after each battle","detail":"The Overland campaign did not end at the Wilderness, Spotsylvania, or the North Anna. Grant repeatedly moved around Lee’s right flank."},{"time":"2:39","audioTime":159,"title":"The armies fought along Totopotomoy Creek","detail":"By May 30, Lee’s lines appeared too costly to attack directly, and Early’s attempt to strike Warren near Bethesda Church had failed."},{"time":"3:18","audioTime":198,"title":"Confederate cavalry probed near Old Cold Harbor","detail":"Matthew Butler’s mounted brigade moved toward Matadequin Creek and met Alfred Torbert’s U.S. cavalry protecting the route to White House Landing."},{"time":"3:50","audioTime":230,"title":"Torbert drove Butler back","detail":"Three U.S. brigades forced Butler’s single brigade away from the creek and pursued it toward Old Cold Harbor."},{"time":"4:34","audioTime":274,"title":"Grant chose another turning movement","detail":"He planned to move beyond Lee’s right with William F. Smith’s XVIII Corps leading from White House Landing."},{"time":"5:05","audioTime":305,"title":"Sheridan received orders to clear the route","detail":"Grant directed Sheridan to move cavalry from the U.S. left toward Cold Harbor and open the way for Smith."},{"time":"5:34","audioTime":334,"title":"XVIII Corps began landing","detail":"About 10,000 men, with more following, disembarked at White House Landing after leaving the Bermuda Hundred lines."},{"time":"6:07","audioTime":367,"title":"Lee requested reinforcements from Beauregard","detail":"Lee warned Jefferson Davis of possible disaster. Beauregard then sent Robert Hoke’s division from Bermuda Hundred."},{"time":"7:24","audioTime":444,"title":"Both commands focused on Cold Harbor","detail":"The five-road crossroads controlled routes toward the Chickahominy crossings and the next U.S. movement around Lee’s right."},{"time":"8:54","audioTime":534,"title":"Fitzhugh Lee organized the Confederate defense","detail":"Butler’s brigade and Fitzhugh Lee’s two cavalry brigades built breastworks across the eastern approaches while waiting for Hoke’s infantry."},{"time":"10:17","audioTime":617,"title":"Clingman’s brigade arrived","detail":"Four North Carolina regiments reinforced the Confederate left shortly before U.S. cavalry attacked about 4 p.m. on May 31."},{"time":"10:30","audioTime":630,"title":"Merritt and Custer attacked dismounted","detail":"Two of Torbert’s brigades advanced with Sharps carbines and Spencer repeaters. The first attack identified the Confederate line and the presence of infantry."},{"time":"11:09","audioTime":669,"title":"Merritt turned the Confederate flank","detail":"Part of his brigade held the front while other troopers moved around Clingman’s exposed and poorly placed North Carolinians."},{"time":"12:03","audioTime":723,"title":"The 51st North Carolina was isolated","detail":"Placed about 500 yards ahead of the main line, the regiment was outflanked. Clingman was with it and could not direct the rest of his brigade."},{"time":"12:38","audioTime":758,"title":"The Confederate position collapsed","detail":"Clingman’s brigade broke, Fitzhugh Lee withdrew, and Custer’s mounted charge accelerated the retreat."},{"time":"13:17","audioTime":797,"title":"U.S. cavalry held the crossroads","detail":"Merritt and Custer had driven away four Confederate brigades with two U.S. brigades; Thomas Devin’s brigade did not join the attack."},{"time":"14:05","audioTime":845,"title":"Sheridan briefly abandoned Cold Harbor","detail":"Reports of more Confederate infantry and the distance to U.S. infantry support led Sheridan to order Torbert out after dark."},{"time":"16:51","audioTime":1011,"title":"Wright received orders to march","detail":"At 9:45 p.m. on May 31, VI Corps left the Totopotomoy line for Cold Harbor."},{"time":"17:35","audioTime":1055,"title":"Lee prepared an ambush","detail":"Richard Anderson’s First Corps marched toward Cold Harbor to join Hoke, clear the crossroads, and strike Smith’s column while it was stretched along the roads."},{"time":"18:43","audioTime":1123,"title":"Meade ordered Sheridan back","detail":"Sheridan received the order about 1 a.m. and reoccupied the crossroads two hours later before the Confederates realized it had been vacant."},{"time":"20:05","audioTime":1205,"title":"Kershaw’s division reached the area first","detail":"Only Kershaw’s troops were ready to attack at dawn. Hoke was supposed to advance when he heard the firing."},{"time":"20:49","audioTime":1249,"title":"About 10,000 Confederates faced 5,000 cavalrymen","detail":"Kershaw and Hoke had roughly twice Sheridan’s manpower, but the U.S. cavalry had earthworks and repeating carbines."},{"time":"21:02","audioTime":1262,"title":"The Confederate attack failed quickly","detail":"Lawrence Keitt advanced without skirmishers. Merritt’s dismounted cavalry held fire until close range and then broke the attack with rapid carbine fire."},{"time":"22:12","audioTime":1332,"title":"A second advance also failed","detail":"Kershaw’s men regrouped but were repulsed again. Wright’s infantry then arrived and secured Sheridan’s position."},{"time":"23:02","audioTime":1382,"title":"The operation had begun as a reconnaissance","detail":"Anderson ordered a reconnaissance in force rather than the coordinated attack Lee expected. Kershaw assigned it to Keitt’s inexperienced brigade."},{"time":"24:21","audioTime":1461,"title":"Keitt was mortally wounded","detail":"He rode conspicuously ahead of the formation and was hit by the first U.S. volley. His 20th South Carolina then collapsed under its first major combat."},{"time":"25:10","audioTime":1510,"title":"Hoke did not advance","detail":"The signal and coordination arrangements failed. Hoke remained in place, Kershaw did not renew the attack, and Anderson’s later arrivals began entrenching."},{"time":"26:05","audioTime":1565,"title":"Lee’s counterstroke ended","detail":"By late morning on June 1, the Confederate effort to recover Cold Harbor and attack the U.S. turning movement had failed."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"blue","branches":[{"commander":"Ulysses S. Grant","command":"General-in-chief, U.S. armies","subordinates":"Planned the movement beyond Lee’s right toward the Chickahominy River"},{"commander":"George G. Meade","command":"Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Ordered Sheridan to return to Cold Harbor and sent VI Corps to reinforce him"},{"commander":"Philip H. Sheridan","command":"Cavalry Corps","subordinates":"Directed the seizure and defense of Cold Harbor"},{"commander":"Alfred T. A. Torbert","command":"First Cavalry Division","subordinates":"Wesley Merritt, George A. Custer, and Thomas C. Devin brigades"},{"commander":"Horatio G. Wright","command":"VI Corps","subordinates":"Marched from the Totopotomoy line and relieved the cavalry on June 1"},{"commander":"William F. “Baldy” Smith","command":"XVIII Corps, Army of the James","subordinates":"Landed at White House and moved toward Cold Harbor"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"red","branches":[{"commander":"Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Moved forces to block Grant’s southeastern turn"},{"commander":"Richard H. Anderson","command":"First Corps","subordinates":"Directed the unsuccessful June 1 operation"},{"commander":"Joseph B. Kershaw","command":"Division, First Corps","subordinates":"Sent Lawrence Keitt’s brigade forward and did not renew the attack"},{"commander":"Robert F. Hoke","command":"Division","subordinates":"Was ordered to support Kershaw but did not advance"},{"commander":"Fitzhugh Lee","command":"Cavalry division","subordinates":"Matthew C. Butler and other cavalry brigades defending Cold Harbor on May 31"},{"commander":"Thomas L. Clingman","command":"Brigade, Hoke’s division","subordinates":"Held the Confederate left before being outflanked by Merritt"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Philip H. Sheridan","role":"Commanded the U.S. cavalry that seized Cold Harbor and repelled the June 1 attack","side":"United States","image":"/images/philip-sheridan.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2017896949/"},{"name":"Alfred T. A. Torbert","role":"Directed the cavalry division that attacked the crossroads on May 31","side":"United States","image":"/images/sheridan-generals.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2009633605/"},{"name":"Wesley Merritt","role":"Planned and executed the flank attack that broke Clingman’s position","side":"United States","image":"/images/sheridan-generals.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2009633605/"},{"name":"George A. Custer","role":"Led a dismounted attack and then ordered a mounted charge during the Confederate withdrawal","side":"United States"},{"name":"William F. Smith","role":"Brought XVIII Corps from Bermuda Hundred to lead Grant’s next turning movement","side":"United States","image":"/images/baldy-smith-cold-harbor.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018670739/"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Recognized the importance of Cold Harbor and moved Anderson and Hoke to block Grant","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/robert-e-lee-1864.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666540/"},{"name":"Richard H. Anderson","role":"Failed to coordinate the Confederate attack on the morning of June 1","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Robert F. Hoke","role":"Transferred from Bermuda Hundred but did not advance in support of Kershaw","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Lawrence M. Keitt","role":"Inexperienced brigade commander who led the failed reconnaissance in force","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[{"name":"Lawrence M. Keitt","side":"Confederacy","role":"Brigade commander, Kershaw’s division","outcome":"Mortally wounded while leading the June 1 advance at Cold Harbor; died the following day"}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/cold-harbor-june-1-3-map.jpg","alt":"Historical battle map of Cold Harbor","caption":"The crossroads and road network explain why both armies concentrated at Cold Harbor.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/gvhs01.vhs00233/"},{"image":"/images/philip-sheridan.jpg","alt":"Portrait of Philip H. Sheridan in 1864","caption":"Sheridan’s cavalry captured the crossroads and then held it until VI Corps arrived.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2017896949/"},{"image":"/images/sheridan-generals.jpg","alt":"Philip Sheridan with cavalry generals including Torbert and Merritt","caption":"Sheridan with senior cavalry officers; Alfred Torbert and Wesley Merritt are among the men pictured.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2009633605/"},{"image":"/images/cold-harbor-battlefield.jpg","alt":"Wartime photograph of the Cold Harbor battlefield","caption":"A period stereograph shows the open ground and fieldworks at Cold Harbor.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666598/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Episode 498 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2025/07/27/civil-war-podcast-episode-498/"},{"label":"National Park Service: Cold Harbor, Grant’s Greatest Regret","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/cold-harbor-grant-s-greatest-regret.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service Cold Harbor battlefield overview","url":"https://www.nps.gov/rich/learn/historyculture/cold-harbor.htm"},{"label":"Library of Congress Cold Harbor battle plan","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/gvhs01.vhs00233/"},{"label":"Library of Congress Philip Sheridan portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2017896949/"},{"label":"Library of Congress Sheridan and cavalry generals photograph","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2009633605/"},{"label":"Library of Congress Cold Harbor battlefield photograph","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666598/"}]}}]}]
